Q: Default config of GPIO&#39;s using device tree - Freescale i.MX6 A: <p>I just tested the following DT on a i.MX6 and Buildroot/Busybox, kernel v4.19.169. Here is the DT snippet that makes it work, <strong>BUT</strong> you can't use the pin anymore in user space. Note, by the way, the tricky syntax of the <code>gpios =</code> property. That one drove me nuts !</p>
<pre><code>&amp;gpio7 {
    pinctrl-names = &quot;default&quot;;
    pinctrl-0 = &lt;&amp;pinctrl_gpio_en&gt;;

    sysstat@11 {
        gpio-hog;
        gpios = &lt;11 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH&gt;;
        output-low;
        line-name = &quot;SYS_STAT&quot;;
    };
};

&amp;iomuxc {
    pinctrl-names = &quot;default&quot;;
    pinctrl-0 = &lt;&amp;pinctrl_gpios&gt;;

        pinctrl_gpio_en: gpiostatgrp {
            fsl,pins = &lt;
                MX6QDL_PAD_GPIO_16__GPIO7_IO11      0x130b0
            &gt;;
        };
};
</code></pre>
<p>0x130b0 means : hysteresis, 100K pull-down, PUE=1 (pull-up enable), PKE=1 (keeper enabled). This is enough to drive the pin low, even if the <code>sysstat@11</code> is missing. But the pin is still configured as an input.</p>
<p>Check pin config (note the <strong>[used]</strong> string) :</p>
<pre><code># gpioinfo 6 | grep &quot;line  11&quot;                                                  
        line  11:      unnamed   &quot;SYS_STAT&quot;  output  active-high [used]         
</code></pre>
<p>An alternate way would be to delete the <code>sysstat@11</code> node to just do the pin muxing and pin configuration. I'm not sure if it's very smart, though.</p>
<p>Yet another option, as the pin is configured as a GPIO by default, is to not alter the DT at all and use a /etc/init.d script instead, with the command <code>gpioset 6 11=0</code>. It works too, but it's not as fast as the DT option.</p>
<p>I think I'll go for the last option.</p>

Q: Set cookie then redirect A: <p><strong>Try this example:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>    &lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;
    var cookieName = 'HelloWorld';
    var cookieValue = 'HelloWorld';
    var myDate = new Date();
    myDate.setMonth(myDate.getMonth() + 12);
    document.cookie = cookieName +&quot;=&quot; + cookieValue + &quot;;expires=&quot; + myDate 
                      + &quot;;domain=.example.com;path=/&quot;;
    &lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to check at compile-time if a = b (where a and b are arguments of type T&amp;) is valid inside a template function? A: <blockquote>
<p>I’m looking for a method that <strong>doesn’t require actually performing the assignment</strong>, ideally a trait or concept-based solution. <em>Is there a way in standard c++ to detect at compile-time whether a = b will compile</em> for a given type T inside a function template?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, with C++20, you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/requires.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>requires</code></a> to check the validity of the expression <code>x= y</code> without actually performing the assignment as you require(pun intended) as shown below. Note that we also need to check <code>is_copy_constructible_v</code> in addition to <code>a=b</code> because of a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/18405291/12002570">design flaw in <code>std::vector</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;class T&gt; 
void foo(T&amp; a, const T&amp; b) {

    constexpr bool hasValueType = requires { typename T::value_type;};
    //for non-containers
    constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITHOUT_VALUETYPE = requires 
    {   a=b ;
        requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
    }  ;
    //for containers with value_type
    constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITH_VALUETYPE = requires 
    {   a=b ;
        requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
        requires std::is_copy_constructible_v&lt;typename T::value_type&gt;;
    }  ;


    if constexpr ( IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITH_VALUETYPE || (!hasValueType &amp;&amp; IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITHOUT_VALUETYPE) )
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;assignable &quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    else
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;not assignable &quot;&lt;&lt;  std::endl;   

}
int main() {
    using X = std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt;;
    X v1, v2;
    foo&lt;X&gt;(v1, v2); // prints not assignable
   using Y = std::vector&lt;int&gt;;
   Y v3, v4;
    foo&lt;Y&gt;(v3,v4);   //prints assignable
    int x{},y{};
    foo&lt;int&gt;(x,y); //prints assignable
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/Ehq7E1d4z" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Working demo</a></p>
<hr />
<h3>Method 2</h3>
<p>You can also use <strong>function template overloading</strong> with <code>concept</code> instead of <code>constexpr if</code> as shown in this <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/GcdY11qq8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">demo</a>.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
constexpr bool hasValueType = requires { typename T::value_type;};
//for non-containers
template&lt;typename T&gt;
 constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITHOUT_VALUETYPE = requires(T &amp;a, const T&amp;b) 
    {   a=b ;
        requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
    }  ;
//for containers with value_type
template&lt;typename T&gt;
constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITH_VALUETYPE = requires(T &amp;a, const T&amp;b)  
{   a=b ;
        requires std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;;
        requires std::is_copy_constructible_v&lt;typename T::value_type&gt;;
}  ;

template&lt;typename T&gt; concept IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE = IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITH_VALUETYPE&lt;T&gt; || (!hasValueType&lt;T&gt; &amp;&amp; IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE_WITHOUT_VALUETYPE&lt;T&gt;) ;
//VERSION WHERE COPY ASSIGNABLE
template&lt;IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE T&gt; 
void foo(T&amp; a, const T&amp; b) {
 std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;assignment possible&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;  
 a = b;                                   

}
template&lt;typename T&gt; void foo(T&amp; a, const T&amp; b)
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;assignment not possible&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
}


int main() {
    using X = std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt;;
    X v1, v2;
    foo&lt;X&gt;(v1, v2); // prints not possible 
   using Y = std::vector&lt;int&gt;;
   Y v3, v4;
    foo&lt;Y&gt;(v3,v4);   //prints possible
    int x{}, y{10};
    foo&lt;int&gt;(x,y);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; std::endl;
}
</code></pre>
